<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/neurons-and-synapses">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This intermediate lesson assumes the foundational lesson on the neuron: the parts of the
    cell, the resting potential, and the idea that neurons speak in electrical spikes. If
    those are unfamiliar, work through that lesson first — here we pick up the spike exactly
    where it ends, at the terminal, and follow it across the gap. Nothing else is required
    beyond a willingness to hold several names at once; the transmitter cast is short.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe the full synaptic event, from arriving spike to cleanup, and name the machinery at each step;</li>
    <li>explain why release is quantal and probabilistic, and why the brain tolerates the uncertainty;</li>
    <li>distinguish ionotropic from metabotropic receptors, and EPSPs from IPSPs;</li>
    <li>explain summation as the neuron's real computation, and termination as the control on message length;</li>
    <li>connect the synapse's adjustability — potentiation, depression, pruning — to learning and memory.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The foundational lesson introduced the neuron and its electrical language. This lesson is the
    connection problem: a spike racing down an axon arrives at the terminal and must cross a gap of
    twenty nanometres that electricity cannot jump by itself. At every one of the brain's roughly
    100 trillion synapses, the signal is converted — electrical to chemical and back — and that
    conversion is far more than plumbing. It is where the brain's flexibility lives: synapses
    strengthen and weaken with use, and that adjustability is the physical substrate of learning.
    It is also where pharmacology bites: the reuptake pumps, receptors and breakdown enzymes of the
    synapse are the targets of nearly every neurological and psychiatric drug. So we will tour the
    whole machine — the cell types, the gap, the release machinery, the receptors, the cleanup, the
    transmitter cast — and end at the plasticity that makes the whole apparatus an organ of memory.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the synaptic event, step by step" widget="equationStep" config={synapseCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the five cards twice. On the first pass,
    follow the signal: spike, calcium, release, receptors, decision. On the second, follow
    the timescales — the fast ionic steps take milliseconds while the slow chemical cascades
    run seconds to minutes, and step 4 (cleanup) is what sets how long the message lasts.
    The whole sequence is the unit of brain computation, repeated about 100 trillion times.
  </p>

  <h2>The cast: cell types and the supporting glia</h2>
  <p>
    Neurons come in three functional kinds — sensory neurons carrying signals from the periphery,
    motor neurons commanding muscles and glands, and <strong>interneurons</strong>, which form the
    connections between and are the overwhelming majority of the brain — and dozens of anatomical
    kinds, of which two families matter most for what follows. <strong>Pyramidal cells</strong>, the
    cortex's principal output neurons, have long apical dendrites that collect inputs across cortical
    layers; <strong>Purkinje cells</strong> of the cerebellum have the most spectacular dendritic
    trees in biology — a single one receives on the order of 100,000 synaptic inputs. Whatever the
    shape, the logic is the same: dendrites collect, the soma integrates, the axon transmits.
    Around and among them run the <strong>glia</strong>, the supporting cast that turned out to be
    genuine characters. <strong>Oligodendrocytes</strong> (in the brain and cord) and
    <strong>Schwann cells</strong> (in the periphery) wrap axons in myelin — the electrical
    insulation that makes fast conduction possible, which the next lesson covers;
    <strong>microglia</strong> are the brain's resident immune cells, constantly surveying,
    pruning and scavenging; and <strong>astrocytes</strong> — named for their star shape — feed
    neurons, mop up potassium and transmitter, help form the blood–brain barrier, and actually
    <em>participate</em> in signalling: they enwrap synapses (making the "tripartite" synapse of
    pre- and post-synaptic elements plus astrocyte), respond to transmitter with their own calcium
    waves, and can release gliotransmitters that modulate the conversation. The neuron doctrine's
    19th-century protagonists mostly dismissed glia as packing material; the last three decades of
    neuroscience have been partly a correction.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the cast is assembled — dendrites collect, the soma integrates,
    the axon transmits, and glia wrap, feed and modulate. Now the connection problem itself:
    what happens when a spike reaches the terminal.
  </p>

  <h2>The gap and the release machinery</h2>
  <p>
    The presynaptic terminal is a chemical factory pressed against a gap. In the central nervous
    system the <strong>synaptic cleft</strong> is about 20 nanometres across (the neuromuscular
    junction's is wider, 30–50 nm); across it, the postsynaptic density clusters the receiving
    receptors. The transmission line is the <strong>synaptic vesicle</strong>: a membrane sphere
    some 40 nm across, loaded with thousands of transmitter molecules (roughly 5,000 for glutamate)
    and docked at the terminal membrane, primed for fusion. The trigger is <strong>calcium</strong>.
    The arriving action potential opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels clustered in the terminal;
    local Ca²⁺ rises sharply; and the calcium sensor protein <strong>synaptotagmin</strong> — in a
    conformational change that takes well under a millisecond — releases the SNARE protein complex
    (synaptobrevin, syntaxin, SNAP-25) that was holding vesicle and membrane apart, allowing fusion.
    Thomas Südhof, whose work mapped this machinery, shared the 2013 Nobel Prize for it. Two
    quantitative facts give the system its character. First, release is <strong>quantal</strong>:
    Bernard Katz showed in the 1950s that transmitter leaves in discrete packets — one vesicle,
    one quantum — and a single CNS vesicle produces a tiny postsynaptic blip of well under a
    millivolt, so a typical excitatory CNS synapse must fire several quanta, or receive several
    synapses firing together, to matter. Second, release is <strong>probabilistic</strong>: many
    central synapses release on only 10–50% of arriving spikes — an inherent uncertainty that the
    brain evidently tolerates, and perhaps uses. The neuromuscular junction, by contrast, floods the
    zone — hundreds of vesicles per spike, guaranteeing the muscle fibre fires — an engineering
    difference between "must absolutely twitch" and "should contribute a vote."
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a synapse is a reliable wire — spike in, spike out,
  every time. Most central synapses release on only 10–50% of arriving spikes, and a single
  vesicle's effect is a whisper well under a millivolt. Reliability is not the design goal:
  the nervous system runs on thousands of such unreliable votes summed together, which is
  why summation, not any single connection, is where the computation happens.</p>

  <h2>Receiving: ionotropic and metabotropic receptors</h2>
  <p>
    On the far side of the cleft, transmitter molecules find two receptor families. <strong>Ionotropic
    receptors</strong> are ion channels that open upon binding — within about a millisecond, for as
    long as transmitter remains bound. Glutamate's AMPA receptors open channels that admit sodium
    and depolarise the cell (an <strong>EPSP</strong>, excitatory postsynaptic potential); GABA's
    GABA<sub>A</sub> receptors open chloride channels that clamp the cell toward hyperpolarisation
    (an <strong>IPSP</strong>). This is the fast lane — precise timing, short messages.
    <strong>Metabotropic receptors</strong> work indirectly: binding activates G-proteins that open
    or close channels, or trigger intracellular cascades, over tens of milliseconds to minutes — the
    slow lane that adjusts states rather than passing bits. Both kinds sit on the postsynaptic
    membrane, and here the synapse hands over to the neuron's real computation:
    <strong>summation</strong>. Each EPSP and IPSP is a whisper (a few tenths of a millivolt); the
    postsynaptic soma is a weighted voting booth, adding up <em>temporal</em> summation (the same
    input firing rapidly, spikes stacking before each decays) and <em>spatial</em> summation (many
    inputs firing together, weighted by their position on the dendritic tree — closer to the soma
    counts more). If the membrane at the axon initial segment crosses threshold, the cell fires its
    own spike; otherwise the votes disperse. A cortical pyramidal cell carries around 10,000 such
    inputs. The brain's computation, repeated across 100 trillion synapses, is literally this vote —
    which is why the next lesson's spike is both the fastest and the least nuanced event in the
    nervous system: all the nuance happened before it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> synapses are not little wires carrying "one thought per
  connection," and neurotransmitters do not map one-to-one onto feelings (no "dopamine = pleasure"
  molecule, despite the shorthand). A transmitter's role is defined by its receptor system and
  circuit context — dopamine in one circuit drives movement, in another motivation, in another
  learning.</p>

  <h2>Termination: the message must end</h2>
  <p>
    Release without cleanup would saturate the cleft within seconds and turn precise signalling into
    mush, so every synapse has a termination mechanism, and the mechanism is drug target number one.
    <strong>Enzymatic degradation</strong>: acetylcholinesterase sits in the neuromuscular junction
    and hydrolyses ACh in about a millisecond — the reason a twitch ends when it ends; inhibit that
    enzyme (nerve agents, some insecticides, and the memory-drug donepezil) and ACh lingers, with
    effects ranging from muscle twitching to (at high doses) fatal paralysis of the breathing
    muscles themselves. <strong>Reuptake</strong>: most central transmitters are pumped straight
    back into the terminal (or into astrocytes) by transporter proteins — serotonin transporter,
    dopamine transporter, glutamate transporters — which is where the selective serotonin reuptake
    inhibitors (SSRIs) act, raising synaptic serotonin by slowing its recycling; cocaine's acute
    effects are the same trick on the dopamine transporter. <strong>Diffusion and spill-over</strong>
    lets transmitter escape to neighbouring synapses, and glia scavenge the remainder. The
    fine-grained rule: the faster the cleanup, the sharper the message; the slower, the longer the
    tail — and the tail is where mood and state (the domain of the slow, modulatory systems) are
    tuned.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> release is quantal and probabilistic; the receiving side is a
    weighted vote between fast ionotropic channels and slow metabotropic cascades. What
    remains is how the message ends — and why ending it is drug target number one.
  </p>

  <h2>The transmitter cast</h2>
  <p>
    The brain's chemical vocabulary is small — under a hundred substances — and organised in tiers.
    The workhorses are the <strong>amino acid transmitters</strong>: <strong>glutamate</strong>, the
    principal excitatory messenger of every fast circuit in the brain, and <strong>GABA</strong>,
    the principal inhibitory brake — their balance is the brain's gain control, and its failure is
    epilepsy (excitation unopposed). The <strong>biogenic amines</strong> are the modulators:
    <strong>dopamine</strong> (movement initiation, reward prediction, motivation — Parkinson's
    disease kills its substantia nigra neurons; addictive drugs hijack its pathways),
    <strong>serotonin</strong> (mood, appetite, sleep, the target of SSRIs),
    <strong>norepinephrine</strong> (arousal, alertness, attention), each released by small cell
    clusters whose axons blanket the whole brain — a broadcast architecture, as if a handful of
    radio stations set the weather for a million local conversations. <strong>Acetylcholine</strong>
    does double duty: the fast messenger at every neuromuscular junction, and a central modulator
    of attention and memory (lost early in Alzheimer's disease). Beyond them, <strong>neuropeptides</strong>
    (endorphins, oxytocin, substance P, hundreds of species) act at even lower concentrations and
    longer timescales, often co-released with the small-molecule transmitter to add emotional and
    metabolic colour. And the oddball: <strong>nitric oxide</strong>, a gas, made on demand, diffusing
    backwards across the cleft to modulate the presynaptic terminal — no vesicles, no receptors in
    the usual sense, a retrograde whisper that broke every rule when discovered in the 1980s and
    earned a Nobel in 1998.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> drugs that act on the synapse simply top up chemicals
  the brain is missing. Nearly all of them work by interfering with a specific step of the
  event this lesson described — blocking reuptake (SSRIs, cocaine), inhibiting the breakdown
  enzyme (nerve agents, donepezil), or blocking the receptors themselves — and each
  interference has different consequences depending on the step, the transmitter and the
  circuit. The synapse is a machine with levers, not a tank to be filled.</p>

  <h2>The adjustable synapse: plasticity</h2>
  <p>
    In 1949 Donald Hebb proposed that when one neuron repeatedly helps fire another, the connection
    between them should strengthen — "cells that fire together, wire together." In 1973 Bliss and
    Lømo found the mechanism in the hippocampus: <strong>long-term potentiation</strong>, a lasting
    boost of synaptic strength following high-frequency stimulation, which is still the best
    candidate for a memory trace. Its machinery is the glutamate system's second receptor type:
    the <strong>NMDA receptor</strong>, a molecular coincidence detector. Its channel is blocked by
    a magnesium ion at resting voltage; only when the cell is already depolarised (by AMPA
    neighbours — i.e., the synapse is active) does the block lift and calcium flood in — so calcium
    entry signals <em>both sides were active at once</em>, the Hebbian condition. The resulting
    calcium cascade inserts more AMPA receptors into the postsynaptic membrane and, via retrograde
    messengers, nudges the presynaptic terminal to release more — the synapse is now stronger, and
    can stay that way for weeks to a lifetime. The mirror process, <strong>long-term depression</strong>,
    weakens synapses that are active while the postsynaptic cell is quiet. Development adds the
    third piece: the young brain overproduces synapses — cortical synapse density peaks in
    childhood at perhaps twice adult levels — and then <strong>prunes</strong> the unused, with
    microglia eating the losers, so that the wiring you end up with is a record of the activity you
    had. Learning, in the end, is not the adding of new hardware but the re-weighting of existing
    connections — and the synapse is where the weights are kept.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why must the synaptic signal be converted from electrical to chemical and back?</li>
    <li>What makes release "quantal" and "probabilistic", and how does the neuromuscular junction differ from a typical CNS synapse?</li>
    <li>An SSRI raises synaptic serotonin. Which step of the synaptic event does it interfere with, and what does that change?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Electricity cannot jump the roughly 20-nanometre cleft by
    itself — the spike opens calcium channels, calcium triggers vesicle fusion, and chemical
    messengers carry the signal the rest of the way. (2) Quantal: transmitter leaves in
    discrete vesicle packets (Katz). Probabilistic: many central synapses release on only
    10–50% of spikes. The neuromuscular junction floods the zone with hundreds of vesicles per
    spike because the muscle fibre absolutely must twitch; a CNS synapse only needs to
    contribute a vote. (3) It blocks the serotonin transporter — reuptake — so transmitter
    lingers in the cleft and the message is prolonged, rather than any new serotonin being
    added.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The synaptic event runs: spike, calcium, vesicle fusion, receptor binding, summation, cleanup.</li>
    <li>Release is quantal (one vesicle, one quantum) and probabilistic (10–50% per spike at many central synapses).</li>
    <li>Ionotropic receptors are the fast lane; metabotropic receptors are the slow lane that adjusts states.</li>
    <li>The postsynaptic cell computes by summation — temporal and spatial, excitatory versus inhibitory — and spikes only if the vote crosses threshold.</li>
    <li>Termination (enzymes, reuptake, diffusion) sets message length and is drug target number one.</li>
    <li>Glutamate and GABA are the workhorses; the amines are broadcast modulators; nitric oxide is the rule-breaking gas.</li>
    <li>Plasticity — LTP, LTD and pruning — re-weights existing connections; learning is re-weighting, not new hardware.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson on the action potential picks up the electrical half this lesson
    deliberately skipped — how the spike itself travels, and what myelin has to do with it —
    and the lesson on neurotransmitters and drugs goes deeper into the chemistry introduced
    by the transmitter cast, mapping which drugs act at which receptors and transporters.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer starts with disease as a window. Because the neuromuscular junction is big,
    accessible and stereotyped, its diseases decode the machinery: <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong>
    is autoantibodies against the muscle's acetylcholine receptors — a fatiguing weakness that
    acetylcholinesterase inhibitors temporarily relieve; <strong>Lambert–Eaton syndrome</strong>
    knocks out the presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels — the release machinery's trigger — and
    <strong>botulinum toxin</strong> cleaves the SNARE proteins themselves, which is why a few
    nanograms of it are lethal and why, in absurdly diluted form, it smooths wrinkles and treats
    dystonias. Next, <strong>the numbers of the vote</strong>: a single excitatory CNS synapse
    contributes perhaps 0.2–1 mV against a spike threshold 15–20 mV above rest — so roughly
    dozens to hundreds of near-synchronous excitatory inputs are needed to fire a pyramidal cell,
    with inhibition able to veto much larger excitatory barrages, a design that makes neurons
    coincidence detectors rather than accumulators. Then, <strong>silent synapses</strong>: many
    young (and some adult) glutamatergic synapses carry NMDA receptors but no AMPA receptors —
    functionally mute until plasticity inserts AMPA, meaning the brain holds latent connections in
    reserve, a finding that has reshaped thinking about critical periods and rehabilitation.
    Finally, the astrocyte frontier: one human astrocyte enwraps on the order of 100,000 synapses,
    responds to them with calcium signals, and releases modulators of its own — whether glia merely
    tune the neural conversation or participate in computation remains one of neuroscience's
    genuinely open questions, and a fair close for this lesson: even the anatomy of the synapse,
    worked out step by step across a century, still has rooms unexplored.
  </p>
